sec.p:
We evaluated held-out test windows using the same “mask → predict → tile” procedure. For each test sequence, we iteratively masked 15 % of positions (2,457 positions) and predicted them until all positions had been covered. We specified the --rc flag to average predictions from both the forward sequence and its reverse complement. We then computed the per-position cross-entropy loss (Equation 1) for each 16k window s as:
(1)
LCEs=∑j=1L[−∑n∈{A,C,G,T}Ij,n(s)ln⁡pj,n(s)]wj(s)∑j=1Lwj(s) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathcal{L}_{C E_{s}}=\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{L}\left[-\sum_{n \in\{\mathrm{~A}, \mathrm{C}, \mathrm{G}, \mathrm{T}\}} \mathbb{I}_{j, n}^{(s)} \ln p_{j, n}^{(s)}\right] w_{j}^{(s)}}{\sum_{j=1}^{L} w_{j}^{(s)}}$$\end{document}
where L =16,384 is the window length, Ij,n(s)∈{0,1}\begin{document}$\mathbb{I}_{j, n}^{(s)} \in\{0,1\}$\end{document} is the one-hot indicator for base n at position j in window s,pj,n(s)\begin{document}$s, p_{j, n}^{(s)}$\end{document} is the model’s predicted probability for base n, and wj(s)\begin{document}$w_{j}^{(s)}$\end{document} is a position-specific weight (exon/re-peat scaling) at that position. The global test loss is the average over all N test sequences:
(2)
LCE=1N∑S=1NLCES \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathcal{L}_{C E}=\frac{1}{N} \sum_{S=1}^{N} \mathcal{L}_{C E_{S}}$$\end{document}
Perplexity is then defined as
(3)
Perplexity=exp⁡(LCE) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathrm{Perplexity} =\exp \left(\mathcal{L}_{C E}\right)$$\end{document}
Finally, we calculated taxon-specific evaluation metrics by summing segment losses for each species and dividing by the number of segments for that species, yielding a species-level loss. In addition to loss and perplexity, we computed and stored a position probability matrix (PPM) for each sequence (x_pred), alongside one-hot inputs (x_true), species labels, and scaling weights for each base pair in a pred.npz file. Finally, we converted these PPMs into information content matrices (ICMs)114,115 to facilitate the identification of TF binding sites.

Let pj,n(s)\begin{document}$p_{j, n}^{(s)}$\end{document} be the PPM probability for nucleotide n ∈ {A, C, G, T} at position j in the 16,384 bp window s. We transform this PPM into an ICM via the following steps. To avoid zeros, we first add a small pseudocount ε, yielding
(4)
p~j,n(s)=pj,n(s)+ε \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \tilde{p}_{j, n}^{(s)}=p_{j, n}^{(s)}+\varepsilon$$\end{document}
and then renormalize across nucleotides so that
(5)
p¯j,n(s)=p~j,n(s)∑m∈{A,C,G,T}p~j,m(s),∑np¯j,n(s)=1 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \bar{p}_{j, n}^{(s)}=\frac{\tilde{p}_{j, n}^{(s)}}{\sum_{m \in\{\mathrm{~A}, \mathrm{C}, \mathrm{G}, \mathrm{T}\}} \tilde{p}_{j, m}^{(s)}}, \sum_{n} \bar{p}_{j, n}^{(s)}=1$$\end{document}
The Shannon entropy at position j is
(6)
Hj(s)=−∑n∈{A,C,G,T}p¯j,n(s)log2⁡(p¯j,n(s)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle H_{j}^{(s)}=-\sum_{n \in\{\mathrm{~A}, \mathrm{C}, \mathrm{G}, \mathrm{~T}\}} \bar{p}_{j, n}^{(s)} \log _{2}\left(\bar{p}_{j, n}^{(s)}\right)$$\end{document}
with larger Hj(s)\begin{document}$H_{j}^{(s)}$\end{document} indicating greater nucleotide diversity (i.e. lower conservation). We then define the perposition information content as
(7)
Cj(s)=log2⁡(4)−Hj(s)=2−Hj(s),Cj(s)∈[0,2] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle C_{j}^{(s)}=\log _{2}(4)-H_{j}^{(s)}=2-H_{j}^{(s)}, C_{j}^{(s)} \in[0,2]$$\end{document}
For logo visualization, each nucleotide’s column height is set to
(8)
hj,n(s)=p¯j,n(s)×Cj(s),∑nhj,n(s)=Cj(s) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle h_{j, n}^{(s)}=\bar{p}_{j, n}^{(s)} \times C_{j}^{(s)}, \sum_{n} h_{j, n}^{(s)}=C_{j}^{(s)}$$\end{document}
Stacking letters in ascending hj,n(s)\begin{document}$h_{j, n}^{(s)}$\end{document} order produces a DNA logo whose total column height reflects the information content, Cj(s)\begin{document}$C_{j}^{(s)}$\end{document}, and whose individual letter heights encode the normalized nucleotide probabilities, p¯j,n(s)\begin{document}$\bar{p}_{j, n}^{(s)}$\end{document}.

To evaluate how precisely Shorkie reconstructs fine-scale positional coverage within individual genes, we analyzed consistency at the bin-level within each gene. First, for each gene g and track j, we derived log2-transformed coverage vectors from the predictions and measured coverages:
(15)
v^g,jraw={log2⁡(y^i,j(ens)+1)}i∈B(g),vg,jraw={log2⁡(yi,j+1)}i∈B(g) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \widehat{v}_{g, j}^{\mathrm{raw}}=\left\{\log _{2}\left(\hat{y}_{i, j}^{(\mathrm{ens})}+1\right)\right\}_{i \in B(g)}, \quad v_{g, j}^{\mathrm{raw}}=\left\{\log _{2}\left(y_{i, j}+1\right)\right\}_{i \in B(g)}$$\end{document}
We computed Pearson r for gene-track pairs with non-trivial variation (both variances > 10−6). To de-emphasize nearly flat profiles, we further required Var(v^g,jraw)>τj\begin{document}$\operatorname{Var}\left(\hat{v}_{g, j}^{\text {raw }}\right)>\tau_{j}$\end{document}, where τj is the 80th percentile of predicted variances across genes for track j. For each gene we summarized:
rgwithin, raw=1|{j:Var(v^g,jraw)>τj}|∑j:Var(v^g,jraw)>τjrg,jbin, raw \begin{document}$$\displaystyle r_{g}^{\text {within, raw }}=\frac{1}{\left|\left\{j: \operatorname{Var}\left(\hat{v}_{g, j}^{\text {raw }}\right)>\tau_{j}\right\}\right|} \sum_{j: \operatorname{Var}\left(\hat{v}_{g, j}^{\text {raw }}\right)>\tau_{j}} r_{g, j}^{\text {bin, raw }}$$\end{document}
An identical procedure was conducted using quantile-normalized and mean-centered vectors to compute rgwithin, norm\begin{document}$r_{g}^{\text {within, norm }}$\end{document}.

To derive per-position importance profiles, we defined the set J of T0 RNA-Seq tracks. For each sequence s, mutation (p, n), and position i, we averaged the log2 fold-change scores across T0 tracks:
(18)
Mi,n(s)=1|J|∑j∈Jlog2⁡FCi,n,j(s) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle M_{i, n}^{(s)}=\frac{1}{|\mathbb{J}|} \sum_{j \in \mathbb{J}} \log _{2} \mathrm{FC}_{i, n, j}^{(s)}$$\end{document}
where i indexes positions and n denotes nucleotides. Next, we zero-mean normalized at each position i across the four nucleotides:
(19)
M~i,n(s)=Mi,n(s)−14∑m∈{A,C,G,T}Mi,m(s) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \widetilde{M}_{i, n}^{(s)}=M_{i, n}^{(s)}-\frac{1}{4} \sum_{m \in\{\mathrm{~A}, \mathrm{C}, \mathrm{G}, \mathrm{~T}\}} M_{i, m}^{(s)}$$\end{document}
Finally, to focus on reference-base contributions, we computed:
(20)
Logoi(s)=∑n∈{A,C,G,T}M~i,n(s)×Hi,n(s) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \operatorname{Logo}_{i}^{(s)}=\sum_{n \in\{\mathrm{~A}, \mathrm{C}, \mathrm{G}, \mathrm{~T}\}} \widetilde{M}_{i, n}^{(s)} \times H_{i, n}^{(s)}$$\end{document}
where Hi,n(s)∈{0,1}\begin{document}$H_{i, n}^{(s)} \in\{0,1\}$\end{document} is the one-hot indicator for the reference base at position i. Finally, we visualized per-position scores log0(s)\begin{document}$\log _{0}^{(s)}$\end{document} as DNA sequence logos (ISM maps), highlighting the magnitude and direction of variant effects across the promoter window.

To compare experimental and predicted gene-level coverages at Shorkie’s native 16 bp resolution, we summed bin values over each gene’s span. For gene g and track j, let ℬ(g) be the set of overlapping 16 bp bins. We define:
(23)
covjexp(g)=∑i∈B(g)yi,jexp,covjpred(g)=∑i∈B(g)yi,jpred \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \operatorname{cov}_{j}^{\exp }(g)=\sum_{i \in \mathcal{B}(g)} y_{i, j}^{\exp }, \quad \operatorname{cov}_{j}^{\mathrm{pred}}(g)=\sum_{i \in \mathcal{B}(g)} y_{i, j}^{\mathrm{pred}}$$\end{document}
where ℬ(g) indexes the bins overlapping gene g. Next, we normalized to reads-per-million (RPM) using each sample’s library size libsizej,
(24)
covjexp, RPM(g)=covjexp(g) libsize j×106,covjpred, RPM(g)=covjpred(g) libsize j×106 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \operatorname{cov}_{j}^{\text {exp, RPM }}(g)=\frac{\operatorname{cov}_{j}^{\text {exp }}(g)}{\text { libsize }_{j}} \times 10^{6}, \quad \operatorname{cov}_{j}^{\text {pred, RPM }}(g)=\frac{\operatorname{cov}_{j}^{\text {pred }}(g)}{\text { libsize }_{j}} \times 10^{6}$$\end{document}
To characterize time-dependent gene expression trajectories in TF-induction experiments, we averaged RPM-normalized coverages across replicate tracks at each time point Tt:
(25)
cov⁡(Tt, exp , RPM )(g)=1|𝕁t|∑j∈𝕁tcovjexp, RPM ⁡(g),cov⁡(Tt, pred,RPM )(g)=1|𝕁t|∑j∈𝕁tcovjpred, RPM ⁡(g) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \overline{\operatorname{cov}}^{\left(T_{t}, \text { exp }, \text { RPM }\right)}(g)=\frac{1}{\left|\mathbb{J}_{t}\right|} \sum_{j \in \mathbb{J}_{t}} \operatorname{cov}_{j}^{\text {exp, RPM }}(g), \quad {\overline{\operatorname{cov}}}^{\left(T_{t}, \text { pred,RPM }\right)}(g)=\frac{1}{\left|\mathbb{J}_{t}\right|} \sum_{j \in \mathbb{J}_{t}} \operatorname{cov}_{j}^{\text {pred, RPM }}(g)$$\end{document}
This yields matched, per-gene coverage trajectories for both experimental measurements and Shorkie predictions.

To quantify motif importance changes during induction, we extracted the normalized importance score matrix Ps,t ∈ ℝL×4 for each promoter window s and timepoint t. We flattened this matrix into a vector vs,t ∈ ℝ4L, and computed pairwise Euclidean distances between timepoints:

(26)

Ds(t1,t2)=‖vs,t1−vs,t2‖2,∀t1,t2∈{1,…,T}
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle D_{s}\left(t_{1}, t_{2}\right)=\left\|\mathbf{v}_{s, t_{1}}-\mathbf{v}_{s, t_{2}}\right\|_{2}, \quad \forall t_{1}, t_{2} \in\{1, \ldots, T\}$$\end{document}



To summarize across all promoter windows, we conducted element-wise averaging:

(27)

D¯(t1,t2)=1S∑s=1SDs(t1,t2)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \bar{D}\left(t_{1}, t_{2}\right)=\frac{1}{S} \sum_{s=1}^{S} D_{s}\left(t_{1}, t_{2}\right)$$\end{document}



where S is the total number of promoter windows. We visualized the resulting mean Euclidean-distance matrix D\begin{document}$D$\end{document} as a heatmap, effectively capturing motif shifts over the TF induction time course.

We predicted how individual variants alter gene-level coverage profiles with Shorkie. For each variant, we (1) extracted a 16,384 bp window centered on the SNP; (2) verified that the reference allele matched the extracted sequence; (3) generated one-hot encodings for both the reference and alternate alleles; and (4) averaged predictions over forward and reverse-complement strands. Within the window, we summed the predicted coverage values over all bins overlapping the annotated gene g’s exons:
(28)
Covref=∑i∈B(g)yref(i),Covalt=∑i∈B(g)yalt(i) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \operatorname{Cov}_{\mathrm{ref}}=\sum_{i \in \mathcal{B}(g)} y_{\mathrm{ref}}(i), \quad \operatorname{Cov}_{\mathrm{alt}}=\sum_{i \in \mathcal{B}(g)} y_{\mathrm{alt}}(i)$$\end{document}
where ℬ(g) indexes the bins overlapping gene g. We then defined the log2 fold-change score as
(29)
log2⁡FCShorkie=log2⁡(Covalt+1)−log2⁡(Covref+1) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \log _{2} \mathrm{FC}_{\text {Shorkie }}=\log _{2}\left(\operatorname{Cov}_{\text {alt }}+1\right)-\log _{2}\left(\operatorname{Cov}_{\text {ref }}+1\right)$$\end{document}
